 "exercises": [
  {
   "id": "a1",
   "source_id": "We now have chemical potential written in terms of the partition function",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Apply",
   "place": {
    "at": "end"
   },
   "cite": "q-to-mu",
   "prompt": "<p>For an ideal gas of $N$ molecules, $\\kQsys = q^N/N!$, where $q$ is the partition function of one molecule. Using $\\kmu = -\\kR\\kT\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial \\ln \\kQsys}{\\partial N}\\right)_{\\kV,\\kT}$ and Stirling's approximation $\\ln N! \\approx N\\ln N - N$, show that $\\kmu = -\\kR\\kT\\ln(q/N)$.</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "hint": "Write $\\ln \\kQsys$ with Stirling's approximation before you differentiate; $q$ depends on $\\kV$ and $\\kT$ but not on $N$.",
    "solution": "<p>Taking the logarithm, $\\ln \\kQsys = N\\ln q - \\ln N!$, and Stirling's approximation gives $\\ln \\kQsys \\approx N\\ln q - N\\ln N + N$. At constant $\\kV$ and $\\kT$ the molecular partition function $q$ is fixed, so</p>$$ \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial \\ln \\kQsys}{\\partial N}\\right)_{\\kV,\\kT} = \\ln q - \\ln N - 1 + 1 = \\ln \\dfrac{q}{N} $$<p>and therefore $\\kmu = -\\kR\\kT\\ln(q/N)$. Since $q$ for a gas at ordinary conditions is far larger than $N$, $\\ln(q/N)$ is positive and the chemical potential of an ideal gas is negative.</p>"
   }
  }
 ],
